<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    NOT sandboxed, deliberately.

    The App Sandbox and Full Disk Access are mutually exclusive in practice: a
    sandboxed app cannot read ~/Library/Messages/chat.db no matter what the user
    grants, because the sandbox denies it before TCC is ever consulted. Reading
    that database is the app's primary job.

    This is a personally-signed app that runs on one Mac and is never shipped
    through the App Store, so the trade is bounded — but it is a real trade and
    should be a decision, not a default. What limits it instead:

      - Hardened Runtime is on (build.sh passes -o runtime), so code injection
        and unsigned library loading are blocked.
      - The app talks to exactly two places: Google's OAuth and API endpoints,
        and the assistant over Tailscale via scp. Both are in the source, neither is
        configurable by anything the app reads.
      - It never executes anything it did not ship: the three extractors in
        Resources/, /usr/bin/python3, /usr/bin/scp, /usr/bin/ssh.

    If sandboxing ever becomes necessary, chat.db access has to move to a
    separate non-sandboxed helper — the app cannot simply gain the entitlement.
  -->

  <!--
    Apple Events, needed to tell Messages to send an approved draft.

    This was false while the app only read. Sending moved into the app
    (Outbox.swift, replacing the imessage-send LaunchAgent) and driving
    Messages is an Apple Event, so under Hardened Runtime this must be true or
    osascript is refused by the runtime with no dialog — the same failure the
    addressbook entitlement below caused for Contacts.

    It is not a general grant: Automation is still per-target and per-app, so
    macOS prompts once for Messages specifically on the first send, and the
    app can drive nothing it has not been approved for. What bounds the
    capability is not this key but the two refusals in Outbox.swift — approved
    status only, and only to a handle already seen in a recent thread.

    The JIT and unsigned-memory exceptions remain absent on purpose. Nothing
    here needs them, and each one is a hole in the Hardened Runtime.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Required to read Contacts, and not optional under Hardened Runtime.

    The resource-access entitlements are not only a sandbox thing: with
    `-o runtime`, an app that asks for Contacts without declaring this is
    refused by the runtime itself, before TCC is reached. CNContactStore
    reports that as CNErrorDomain 100 "Access Denied" — the same code a user
    who clicked Don't Allow produces — and no consent dialog is ever shown.

    That cost an hour here: the grant looked like a stale TCC record, so it was
    reset four times, the app was rebuilt and relaunched, and every attempt
    failed identically because nothing about TCC was ever wrong. The tell is
    that the app never appears in the Contacts list in System Settings — an app
    denied by the runtime never gets far enough to be listed.

    Full Disk Access is unaffected, which is why iMessage and Calls worked
    throughout: FDA has no corresponding entitlement and is pure TCC.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    Reminders, for the Apple Reminders bridge (Reminders.swift). Same story as
    the addressbook key above: under Hardened Runtime an app that asks for
    EventKit without declaring this is refused by the runtime before TCC is
    consulted, and no dialog appears. Calendars and reminders share this one
    key; the app only ever asks for .reminder.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
